Getting started with Copilot code review (public preview)

Copilot code review gives you fast, actionable AI feedback on your code so you can start iterating towards "ready to merge" while you wait for a human reviewer. There are three ways to get a review from Copilot:

- Tag Copilot as a pull request reviewer on GitHub.com from the "Reviewers" menu: <%= Copilot::CODE_REVIEW_REQUEST_DOCS_URL %>
- Enable automatic reviews for new pull requests on GitHub.com using rulesets (admin permissions required): <%= Copilot::CODE_REVIEW_AUTOMATIC_DOCS_URL %>
- Use the "Review Changes" button in Visual Studio Code, available when drafting a commit in the "Source Control" tab: <%= Copilot::CODE_REVIEW_VS_CODE_DOCS_URL %>

Note that Copilot code review only supports a limited number of programming languages, and isn't guaranteed to spot all code problems, so you should still make sure you get a human review. For more information on Copilot code review, head to the docs: https://gh.io/copilot-code-review-docs.

Taking Copilot's suggestions forward with Copilot Workspace on GitHub.com

Alongside Copilot code review, you also have access to Copilot Workspace, a new experience on GitHub.com where you can validate and tweak Copilot's suggestions before applying them to your branch.

To jump into Workspace, just click the "Open in Workspace" on a suggestion from Copilot code review on GitHub.com, or use the "Edit" button on any file on GitHub. For more information on Copilot Workspace, head to the docs: <%= Copilot::WORKSPACE_FOR_PRS_DOCS_URL %>.

